## Level I status

**STILL OPEN.** This report excludes a specified source class. It does not obtain
either of the global quantifiers required for Level I closure.

## 1. Starting endpoint and lane

The canonical global problem is open. In particular, the macroscopic inactive
population cannot be discarded while improving its old diameter-carrying core.
The canonical regular-shell regression in shared theory §§13–14 shows that local
optimality, KKT stress, and leading hexagonal density can coexist with a quadratic
contact-isolated interior. It does not certify global optimality.

Open PRs inspected before choosing this lane: #578 (an affine chart for the whole
source), #579 (loaded-hull lattice-shadow obstruction), #581 (diameter cover and
vacancy refill), #582 (fixed-index straightening regression), and the titles/scopes
of #583–588 (harmonic outer-walk/cap charges). This argument allows every hull
vertex to be a lattice-chart outlier. It counts missing *interior* chart sites and
changes the index set of the competitor.

```text
almost unit hull + one near-exact lattice chart for almost all actual interior points
    -> a positive linear deficit below area / (sqrt(3)/2)
    -> translated triangular-lattice disk with the same cardinality and diameter < D
    -> exclusion of this source class from global minimizers
```

The missing lattice sites are not vacant source sites. No simultaneous insertion
into the old source is asserted or used.

## 2. Main theorem

Put

\[
a_0=\sqrt3/2,\qquad r_0=(1-a_0)/2,
\qquad \mu_0=\frac\pi6-\frac{\sqrt3}4,
\qquad \delta_0=2\mu_0=\frac\pi3-\frac{\sqrt3}2.
\]

Consider a sequence of finite unit-separated sources \(A_j\), of cardinalities
\(n_j\), diameters \(D_j\to\infty\), and two-dimensional hulls \(K_j\).
Assume:

1. The actual source hull has perimeter \(P_j\), vertex count \(q_j\), and
   **perimeter excess** \(P_j-q_j=o(D_j)\). Equivalently, the sum of
   \(\ell_i-1\) over all hull sides is sublinear. All hull sides have length
   at least one, by source separation. Write the vertices in cyclic order as
   \(p_0,\ldots,p_{q_j-1}\). Exact unit sides are a special case; no side
   is required to be an exact contact.
2. After a translation,
   \(D_j^{-1}K_j\to B(0,1/2)\) in Hausdorff distance.
3. Apart from \(t_j=o(D_j)\) exceptional non-hull points, every actual interior
   source point has an injective lattice shadow at distance
   at most \(\epsilon_j\to0\), in one lattice
   \(\Lambda_j=b_j+\lambda_j U_j\mathbb Z^2\). The columns of \(U_j\)
   are unit vectors with angle in \([\pi/3,2\pi/3]\), and
   \(1\le\lambda_j\le L\) for one fixed finite \(L\).

Then

\[
\boxed{\liminf_j
\frac{\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)-n_j}{D_j}
\ge\frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}>0.}
\tag{2.1}
\]

There is consequently a same-cardinality unit-separated triangular-lattice
competitor, with unrestricted new indices, and

\[
\boxed{\liminf_j\bigl(D_j-D(n_j)\bigr)\ge\delta_0>0.}
\tag{2.2}
\]

In particular, such a sequence cannot consist of global minimizers. Triangle
freeness is not needed for this conclusion. Every contact-isolated source point
is retained in the cardinality; an isolated point must have a shadow or be counted
among the exceptional points.

Here \(\delta_0=0.1811721474\ldots\) and
\(\pi\mu_0/a_0=0.3286099257\ldots\). The positive constant is a strict-competitor margin for this class, not an
improvement to a carrier coefficient. No effective threshold in \(D\) is claimed.

## 3. Proof: geometry and counting

The one-chart proof and its physical atlas extensions are given in full, including the equidistribution step that must not be
replaced by an assumption of a generic translation. First, §§3.1–3.7 prove the
result under the stronger condition that the total length of nonunit sides is
\(o(D_j)\). Section 3.8 then proves the stated perimeter-excess version,
including sources in which every hull side is slightly longer than one.

### 3.1 Unit hull sides exclude a boundary layer of depth a_0

Let \(x\in A_j\) be a non-hull point and let \(y\) be a closest point of
\(\partial K_j\). If \(y\) is a vertex, then \(|x-y|\ge1\). Otherwise suppose
\(y\) lies on a unit side with endpoints \(u,v\), and \(xy\) is perpendicular
to that side. The closer endpoint is at distance at most

\[
\sqrt{\operatorname{dist}(x,\partial K_j)^2+1/4}.
\]

Unit separation therefore gives

\[
x\in (K_j)_{-a_0}
=\bigcap_{\nu}\{x:x\cdot\nu\le h_{K_j}(\nu)-a_0\}.
\tag{3.1}
\]

For each point satisfying (3.1), a lattice shadow lies in \((K_j)_{-(a_0-\epsilon_j)}\), by the
same half-plane inequalities. No other source point can lie in the interior of
a unit hull side, since its distance to a side endpoint would be less than one.
If the closest boundary point instead lies on a nonunit side, an interior point
with distance less than \(a_0\) lies in that side's radius-\(a_0\) capsule.
Disjoint radius-\(1/2\) disks at these source points bound their number by
\(C(\ell+1)\) for a side of length \(\ell\). Every nonunit side has
\(\ell\ge1\), so summing gives \(O(P_{\rm bad})=o(D_j)\) exceptions,
where \(P_{\rm bad}\) is the total nonunit side length. All other interior
points satisfy (3.1). The uncharted \(t_j\) points are also retained explicitly
in the count. A shadow is needed only for the remaining interior points.

### 3.2 Consequences of the normalized disk limit

Write \(R_j=D_j/2\) and let \(\phi_i\) denote exterior turning angles.
Hausdorff convergence of convex bodies gives uniform convergence of support
functions, so \(h_{K_j}/R_j\to1\). The following consequences are uniform:

\[
q_j=\operatorname{per}(K_j)+o(R_j)=2\pi R_j+o(R_j),
\qquad \max_i\phi_i\to0.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Here is a direct verification. Between successive edge-normal directions the
support function is the scalar product with one vertex, hence satisfies
\(h''+h=0\). At an edge normal its derivative has an upward jump equal to that
edge's length. In distributional notation,

\[
h+h''=\sum_i |p_{i+1}-p_i|\,\delta_{\nu_i}
=\sum_i\ell_i\delta_{\nu_i}.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Pairing with the constant function gives \(P_j=\int h=2\pi R_j+o(R_j)\).
Since the nonunit side count is at most \(P_{\rm bad}\), we have
\(q_j=P_j-P_{\rm bad}+\#\{\text{nonunit sides}\}=P_j+o(D_j)\).
Pairing with a smooth periodic test function \(f\) gives

\[
\frac1{P_j}\sum_i \ell_i f(\nu_i)
=\frac1{P_j}\int h(f+f'')
\longrightarrow \frac1{2\pi}\int f.
\tag{3.4}
\]

Approximation extends (3.4) to every continuous test function. The nonunit edges have total weight \(o(P_j)\). Thus the unit edge
directions, counted once each, are asymptotically uniformly distributed in angle. If a vertex had
an exterior angle bounded below, a convergent subsequence of its normalized
position and two incident support normals would give two distinct normals at
one boundary point of the limit disk. A disk has a unique normal there. This
proves the second assertion of (3.2).

For fixed bounded \(s\ge0\), all offset sides remain nonempty for large \(j\):
their lengths are
\(\ell_i-s(\tan(\phi_i/2)+\tan(\phi_{i+1}/2))\), with \(\ell_i\ge1\).
Consequently the elementary polygon-offset formula gives

\[
\operatorname{area}((K_j)_{-s})
=\operatorname{area}(K_j)-s P_j+s^2\sum_i\tan(\phi_i/2)
=\operatorname{area}(K_j)-s P_j+O(1).
\tag{3.5}
\]

To obtain the formula, the perimeter after offset \(s\) is
\(P_j-2s\sum\tan(\phi_i/2)\); integrating this perimeter from zero to \(s\)
subtracts the swept area. The sum of tangents tends to \(\pi\), since
\(\sum\phi_i=2\pi\) and \(\max\phi_i\to0\).

### 3.3 A lattice count with an o(D) boundary error

For the compact family of lattices in the theorem, and any convex regions
\(Q_j\) with the same normalized disk limit, put
\(a_j=\lambda_j^2\sin\theta_j\ge a_0\). Then

\[
\#(\Lambda_j\cap Q_j)
\le \operatorname{area}(Q_j)/a_j+o(D_j).
\tag{3.6}
\]

This requires more than the usual cell-cover \(O(D)\) error. Let
\(H_j=\operatorname{conv}(\Lambda_j\cap Q_j)\), and let \(B_j\) be the number
of lattice sites on its boundary. Covering radii are uniformly bounded. The regions \(Q_j\) contain disks of
radius \(D_j/2-o(D_j)\). Taking nearest lattice sites to points in a fixed
constant erosion of these disks shows that \(H_j/D_j\) has the same disk limit. In particular its longest side is \(o(D_j)\): a side of
linear length would converge to a nontrivial straight segment on a disk boundary.

Write each oriented hull side as a positive integer multiple of a primitive
lattice vector. Primitive vectors of length at least \(M\) contribute at most
\(\operatorname{per}(H_j)/M=O(D_j/M)\) to \(B_j\). For fixed \(M\) there are
uniformly finitely many shorter primitive vectors, because the lattice matrices
and their inverses are bounded. Each direction occurs on at most one oriented
hull side, contributes \(o(D_j)\), and the shortest lattice vector has length
at least one. First let \(j\to\infty\), then \(M\to\infty\). This proves
\(B_j=o(D_j)\).

Triangulate \(H_j\) using all its lattice sites as vertices. If there are \(I_j\)
interior sites, Euler's formula gives \(2I_j+B_j-2\) triangles. A nondegenerate
lattice triangle has area at least \(a_j/2\), by its integer determinant. Thus
\(\operatorname{area}(H_j)/a_j\ge I_j+B_j/2-1\), and
\(I_j+B_j\le\operatorname{area}(Q_j)/a_j+B_j/2+1\). This proves (3.6).

The assertion about the shortest vector follows directly from
\(m^2+n^2+2mn\cos\theta\ge m^2+n^2-|mn|\ge1\) for nonzero integer
\((m,n)\), and multiplication by \(\lambda_j\ge1\).

### 3.4 An inward trace is uniformly equidistributed modulo the lattice

At \(p_i\), choose the outward unit normal \(N_i\) bisecting its normal cone.
Put

\[
t_0=(1+a_0)/2,\qquad z_i=p_i-t_0N_i.
\]

For every fixed \(0<r<r_0\), uniformly over the allowed lattice translations,
rotations, angles, and scales,

\[
\frac1{q_j}\sum_i\#(\Lambda_j\cap B(z_i,r))
=\frac{\pi r^2}{a_j}+o(1).
\tag{3.7}
\]

The balls are open. Their radius is less than \(1/2\), so each contains at most
one lattice point. The conclusion includes adversarial translations.

Here is a proof of (3.7). Write the lattice as \(b_j+M_j\mathbb Z^2\).
For a nonzero integer vector \(m\), its physical character frequency is
\(k_j=M_j^{-T}m\). It stays in a compact set separated from zero. To prove
equidistribution, it suffices to show

\[
\frac1{q_j}\sum_i
e^{2\pi i k_j\cdot(z_i-b_j)}\longrightarrow0.
\tag{3.8}
\]

Pass to any subsequence on which \(k_j\to k\ne0\). For each fixed integer
\(h>0\), small exterior angles imply, outside at most \(h\) times the nonunit side count
(which is \(o(q_j)\)),

\[
z_{i+h}-z_i=h u_i+o(1),\qquad u_i=p_{i+1}-p_i.
\tag{3.9}
\]

Indices are cyclic. On these good index windows every intervening edge is unit.
Their directions differ by at
most \(h\max\phi_i\), and the normal difference tends to zero. By (3.4), the
character correlation tends to

\[
C_h=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}
e^{2\pi i h k\cdot(\cos v,\sin v)}\,dv.
\tag{3.10}
\]

Moreover \(C_h\to0\) as \(h\to\infty\). To verify this without a Bessel
asymptotic, rotate \(k\) to the horizontal axis. Remove arcs of angular width
\(\eta\) around the two zeros of the phase derivative. Their contribution is
\(O(\eta)\). Integration by parts on the remaining arcs bounds their
contribution by \(O((h|k|\eta^2)^{-1})\). Choosing \(\eta=h^{-1/3}\) proves
convergence to zero.

For unit-modulus cyclic numbers \(w_i\), averaging the \(H\) cyclic shifts and
applying Cauchy–Schwarz gives the exact inequality

\[
\left|q^{-1}\sum_i w_i\right|^2
\le\frac1H+\frac2{H^2}\sum_{h=1}^{H-1}(H-h)
\operatorname{Re}\left(q^{-1}\sum_i w_{i+h}\overline{w_i}\right).
\tag{3.11}
\]

Apply it to the characters in (3.8), take \(j\to\infty\), and bound real parts
by \(|C_h|\). The omitted index windows contribute \(o(1)\) for each fixed
\(h\), because their character correlations have modulus at most one. The right side tends to zero as \(H\to\infty\), since the
Cesàro average of \(|C_h|\) tends to zero. This proves (3.8). Translations
only multiply the character by a unit complex number; they never change this
bound.

Trigonometric polynomial approximation now proves convergence against continuous
functions on the lattice torus. The indicator of a radius-\(r\) ball modulo a
lattice has mean \(\pi r^2/a_j\) and boundary of zero torus area. Continuous
inner and outer approximations therefore give (3.7). Uniformity for varying
lattice matrices follows by compactness: extract \(M_j\to M\); in a fixed
fundamental square the corresponding ellipses can be sandwiched between the
limit ellipses with radii \(r\pm\eta\). Only finitely many lattice translates
meet this square, and the annular area tends to zero with \(\eta\). Thus a
purported failure of uniformity would contradict the subsequence proof above.

### 3.5 These sites are excluded, distinct, and inside the counted region

For fixed \(r<r_0\) and large \(j\),

\[
B(z_i,r)\subset (K_j)_{-(a_0-\epsilon_j)},
\qquad B(z_i,r+\epsilon_j)\subset B(p_i,1).
\tag{3.12}
\]

The second inclusion follows from \(t_0+r<1\). For the first, the support-line
clearance of \(z_i\) is at least \(t_0-o(1)\). To check this uniformly, consider
any support line with normal \(\nu\). Its clearance equals

\[
h_{K_j}(\nu)-p_i\cdot\nu+t_0N_i\cdot\nu.
\]

The first term is nonnegative. Along any subsequence on which \(\nu-N_i\to0\),
the last term tends to \(t_0\). If the two normals instead remain separated,
normalize by \(R_j\) and use the disk limit: the first term has a positive
linear lower bound, since both vertex position and its normal converge to the
same point/direction on a circle. These alternatives prove the asserted
clearance. Since \(t_0-r>a_0\), (3.12) follows.

The target balls are disjoint for large \(j\). Otherwise two centers would be
within \(2r\), and their original vertices would be at bounded distance. In the
normalized disk limit their positions coincide, hence their outward normals
converge to the same normal. It follows that
\(|z_i-z_l|\ge|p_i-p_l|-o(1)\ge1-o(1)>2r\), a contradiction.

A lattice site in one of these balls cannot be the shadow of an actual interior
source point. Such a source point would, by (3.12), lie at distance less than one
from \(p_i\). Thus (3.7) provides

\[
M_j=q_j\pi r^2/a_j+o(D_j)
\tag{3.13}
\]

distinct missing sites inside the very same offset region used in (3.6).

### 3.6 Population deficit and a genuine strict competitor

Apply (3.5), (3.6), and (3.13) to the injective shadows of the covered interior
points. The points near nonunit sides contribute \(o(D_j)\), and all uncharted
points contribute \(t_j\):

\[
n_j\le q_j+t_j+
\frac{\operatorname{area}(K_j)-a_0P_j-q_j\pi r^2}{a_j}+o(D_j).
\tag{3.14}
\]

The numerator is positive for large \(j\), because area is quadratic and
\(q_j\) is linear. Since \(a_j\ge a_0\), replacing its denominator by \(a_0\), using
\(q_j\le P_j\), and retaining \(t_j=o(D_j)\) gives

\[
n_j\le\operatorname{area}(K_j)/a_0-q_j\pi r^2/a_0+o(D_j)
\le\frac{\pi D_j^2}{4a_0}-\frac{\pi^2r^2}{a_0}D_j+o(D_j).
\tag{3.15}
\]

The last step uses the Euclidean isodiametric inequality and (3.2). Let
\(r\uparrow r_0\) *after* taking the lower limit. This proves the preliminary ball version of (2.1), with coefficient
\(\pi^2r_0^2/a_0\), without
assuming a convergence rate for roundness, chart error, or turning angles.

For completeness, average translations of the unit triangular lattice in a disk
of radius \(\rho=\sqrt{a_0 n_j/\pi}\). The mean number of sites is exactly
\(\pi\rho^2/a_0=n_j\). Some translation therefore contains at least \(n_j\)
sites. Select any \(n_j\) of them. Every separation is at least one and their
diameter is at most \(2\rho\). The theorem has thus constructed a competitor
with the full source cardinality. No old diameter constraint has to survive.

This preliminary estimate implies

\[
2\sqrt{a_0n_j/\pi}\le D_j-2\pi r_0^2+o(1).
\]

One can avoid manipulating the lower-limit error by using (3.15) for each fixed
\(r<r_0\) first and then increasing \(r\). This first proves a strict gap of \(2\pi r_0^2\). The next subsection proves
the stronger constants stated in (2.1)–(2.2).

### 3.7 The full scalloped strip gives the stated stronger gap

The inward balls deliberately count only a small portion of the missing strip.
One can count almost all of that strip, without assuming a favorable lattice
phase, by strengthening §3.4 to **joint** equidistribution of side-center phase
and side direction.

For each unit side write \(c_i=(p_i+p_{i+1})/2\), let \(u_i\) be its directed
unit tangent, and let \(\nu_i\) be its outward normal. For every continuous
function \(g\) on the direction circle and every nonzero lattice character,

\[
q_j^{-1}\sum_{i:\ell_i=1}g(u_i)
 e^{2\pi i k_j\cdot(c_i-b_j)}\longrightarrow0.
\tag{3.16}
\]

To prove it, apply (3.11) to
\(w_i=g(u_i)e^{2\pi i k_j\cdot(c_i-b_j)}\), with zero values at nonunit
sides. The same identity holds for these bounded, not necessarily unit-modulus,
values, with diagonal term \(H^{-1}q_j^{-1}\sum|w_i|^2\).
Outside \(o(q_j)\) bad windows, for each fixed h,
\(c_{i+h}-c_i=h u_i+o(1)\) and \(g(u_{i+h})-g(u_i)=o(1)\).
The correlation therefore tends to

\[
(2\pi)^{-1}\int |g(u(v))|^2 e^{2\pi i h k\cdot u(v)}\,dv.
\]

This tends to zero as h tends to infinity. Approximate the continuous weight
\(|g|^2\) uniformly by a smooth weight, then use the integration-by-parts
argument in §3.4. The diagonal term tends to zero as H increases. This proves
(3.16). For the zero character, (3.4) supplies the uniform direction marginal.
Trigonometric polynomial approximation on the product torus consequently proves
that side-center phase and direction converge jointly to Haar measure on the
lattice torus times uniform angular measure. Lattice matrices can vary in the
same compact family; the subsequence and boundary-sandwich argument of §3.4
still applies. The fact that c_i itself has size D does not require a bound on
(M_j-M)c_i: all character estimates used the actual frequency k_j and the
bounded fixed-h differences, not the absolute phases.

For fixed \(\eta>0\), sufficiently small, use the following open patch in
side coordinates, with positive y pointing inward:

\[
F_\eta=\left\{(s,y): |s|<\tfrac12-\eta,\quad
 a_0+\eta<y<\sqrt{1-(\tfrac12-|s|)^2}-\eta\right\}.
\tag{3.17}
\]

Empty vertical intervals contribute nothing. Place it at each unit side as
\(c_i+s u_i-y\nu_i\). Its area tends, as \(\eta\downarrow0\), to

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_0
&=2\int_0^{1/2}(\sqrt{1-v^2}-a_0)\,dv\\
&=\frac{\sqrt3}4+\frac\pi6-a_0
=\frac\pi6-\frac{\sqrt3}4.
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.18}
\]

For fixed eta these patches have the following three properties for large j:

1. They lie in \((K_j)_{-(a_0-\epsilon_j)}\). For a support normal approaching
   \(\nu_i\), the clearance of a patch point is at least \(y-o(1)>a_0\):
   the tangential correction is \(o(1)\). If that normal stays separated, the
   disk limit gives a positive linear support gap, as in §3.5. This proves the
   inclusion uniformly over the entire patch.
2. Every patch point, and every point within \(\epsilon_j\) of it, is less
   than one from one side endpoint. Its squared distance to the closer endpoint
   is \((1/2-|s|)^2+y^2\). The upper bound on y makes this at most
   \(1-2a_0\eta+\eta^2<1\). A fixed eta therefore leaves a uniform positive
   margin for the chart error.
3. Distinct patches are disjoint. If two patches intersect, their side centers
   have bounded distance. Their normalized positions then coincide on the
   disk, and their outward normals approach the same normal. On the shorter
   boundary-normal interval joining them, all tangents approach one direction.
   Their center displacement in that tangent direction is
   \(\ell_i/2+\sum\ell_k+\ell_l/2+o(1)\ge1-o(1)\); bounded center distance
   bounds that intermediate arc length, since its tangents are almost parallel.
   The two patch tangent intervals have total width \(1-2\eta\), and their
   bounded inward depths change the tangent projections by \(o(1)\).
   They cannot intersect. This also handles unit sides separated by nonunit
   sides; each intermediate side has length at least one.

The function that counts lattice sites in an oriented translated \(F_\eta\)
is periodic in the center phase, uniformly bounded, and has a discontinuity
set of zero measure on the product torus. Its boundaries are finitely many
line/circle arcs. At every fixed orientation its mean over lattice translations
is \(\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)/a_j\). Joint equidistribution therefore gives

\[
M_j=q_j\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)/a_j+o(D_j)
\tag{3.19}
\]

distinct lattice sites, inside the counted erosion, that cannot be shadows of
source points. Indicator approximation and uniformity for varying lattice
matrices follow by the same compact-family argument as (3.7); rotated patch
boundaries still have zero area. This step counts actual excluded chart sites,
not a virtual determinant or an average over replacement sources.

Replace \(\pi r^2\) by \(\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)\) in (3.14)–(3.15).
First take j to infinity for fixed eta, then let eta decrease to zero. The
result is

\[
\liminf\frac{\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)-n_j}{D_j}
\ge\frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}.
\]

Translation averaging of the independent triangular-lattice disk now gives
\(\liminf(D_j-D(n_j))\ge2\mu_0\), exactly (2.1)–(2.2). All pairwise
separations and the cardinality are verified by the disk construction in §3.6.

### 3.8 Perimeter excess suffices; exact unit sides are unnecessary

Write \(E_j=P_j-q_j=\sum_i(\ell_i-1)=o(D_j)\). Fix a small \(\alpha>0\).
Call a side good if \(1\le\ell_i\le1+\alpha\). The remaining sides have total
length at most \((1+1/\alpha)E_j=o(D_j)\). Points within a bounded-width strip
at these sides contribute \(o(D_j)\), by the capsule packing count in §3.1.

For a good side, the closest-endpoint estimate gives an interior point's
boundary clearance at least \(\sqrt{1-\ell_i^2/4}\). For \(1\le\ell\le2\),

\[
\sqrt{1-\ell^2/4}\ge a_0(2-\ell),
\]

because the difference of the squares is \((\ell-1)(2-\ell)\ge0\).
Thus all other covered source points have shadows in
\((K_j)_{-(a_0(1-\alpha)-\epsilon_j)}\). Formula (3.5) is valid: every original
side still has length at least one and every turn tends to zero.

The joint phase/direction argument remains valid with nonunit edge lengths.
The difference between length-weighted and vertex-counted direction measures
is bounded by \(E_j/q_j=o(1)\). For each fixed h the following averaged error
bound replaces the uniform good-window estimate:

\[
q_j^{-1}\sum_i|c_{i+h}-c_i-h u_i|
\le h E_j/q_j+C h^2(\max\phi_i)P_j/q_j=o(1),
\tag{3.20}
\]

where now \(u_i=(p_{i+1}-p_i)/\ell_i\). To verify it, express the center
increment as half the first and last edge vector plus the full intervening
edge vectors. Their lengths differ from one by nonnegative amounts; each
length excess is counted h times in the sum over i. Their directions differ
from u_i by at most \((h+1)\max\phi_i\). This yields (3.20), with an absolute
constant. Continuous direction weights vary by o(1), and the character map
is Lipschitz at a fixed bounded frequency. These facts suffice for every
correlation limit in (3.16), hence for joint equidistribution.

Fix a patch parameter \(\eta>0\), then take alpha sufficiently small relative
to eta, for example \(\alpha< a_0\eta/2\). Place the same reference patch
\(F_\eta\) at each good side's midpoint. Its closer endpoint has tangential
distance \(\ell_i/2-|s|\), rather than \(1/2-|s|\). The squared-distance
increase is at most \(\alpha/2+\alpha^2/4\), whereas the original exclusion
margin is at least \(2a_0\eta-\eta^2\). Thus each patch and its vanishing
chart-error neighborhood remain inside one endpoint's open unit disk.
The support-clearance and disjointness proofs in §3.7 are unchanged: good
side lengths are at least one, and the reference tangent width is still
\(1-2\eta\). The patches lie inside the erosion being counted. Omitting the
bad sides removes only o(D) uniformly bounded lattice counts. Consequently
(3.19) still holds.

Counting the covered shadows, the uncharted points, and the o(D) points near
bad sides gives

\[
\begin{aligned}
n_j
&\le q_j+t_j+
 \frac{\operatorname{area}(K_j)-a_0(1-\alpha)P_j
       -q_j\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)}{a_j}+o(D_j)\\
&\le \operatorname{area}(K_j)/a_0+\alpha P_j
       -q_j\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)/a_0+t_j+o(D_j).
\end{aligned}
\tag{3.21}
\]

The second line uses positivity of the quadratic numerator, \(a_j\ge a_0\),
and \(q_j\le P_j\). Therefore

\[
\liminf\frac{\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)-n_j+t_j}{D_j}
\ge\frac{\pi\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)}{a_0}-\pi\alpha.
\]

For each fixed eta let alpha decrease to zero after j tends to infinity. Then
let eta decrease to zero. This proves the exact constants of (2.1) and (2.2)
under the perimeter-excess hypothesis, and also proves the exception allowance
in §4.1. There is no exchange of an uncontrolled convergence rate with D.

## 4. Application to actual global minimizers

The disk-limit hypothesis holds for actual global minimizers by the canonical
source-roundness and Oler inputs, without a stress or core-population hypothesis.
Here is the precise dependency chain:

1. Triangular-lattice translation averaging gives
   \(n\ge\pi D(n)^2/(4a_0)\). The non-strict version suffices.
2. Canonical shared theory Lemma 21.1 and perimeter \(\le\pi D\) give
   \(\operatorname{area}(K)\ge\pi D^2/4-O(D)\).
3. Canonical ambient-force module §1 explicitly records the *full source* bound
   \(K\subset B(0,D/2+3^{1/4}\sqrt D)\). It does not require the extra
   smaller-optimum embedding hypothesis of its Target A source.
4. These two bounds force \(K/D\to B(0,1/2)\). Otherwise a support line would
   omit a fixed positive-area disk cap after normalization, contradicting the
   area lower bound. Compactness supplies such a cap along any nonconvergent
   subsequence.

Hence, for every fixed \(L\), there cannot be global minimizers of unbounded
cardinality whose hull perimeter excess \(P-q\) is \(o(D)\), and whose actual interior
admits the specified chart with error tending to zero apart from \(o(D)\)
exceptional points. This statement concerns the actual
source, including isolated points, rather than its harmonic carrier.

The bounded-scale restriction is harmless if one wants to apply the corollary
to charts initially allowed arbitrary inflation: if \(\lambda_j\to\infty\),
disjoint radius-\(\lambda_j/2\) disks at the interior lattice shadows bound
their number by \(O((D_j/\lambda_j+1)^2)\). Hull vertices and uncharted exceptions number \(O(D_j)\).
This contradicts the required quadratic source population. Thus a subsequence
of global minimizers has bounded inflation, or is already impossible by this
coarse count.

Regular unit polygons with the canonical lattice fillings satisfy these
hypotheses, as do arbitrary unit-sided near-round convex hulls. Unlike the
canonical leading-density construction, this theorem pays an explicit
boundary-order loss and proves a strict diameter comparison.

### 4.1 A quantified allowance for uncharted interior points

The same proof retains an arbitrary exceptional population \(t_j\) in (3.14).
For each fixed \(\eta>0\), replace the coefficient in the next display by
\(\pi\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)/a_0\); then let eta decrease to zero:

\[
\liminf_j\frac{\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)-n_j+t_j}{D_j}
\ge\frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}.
\tag{4.1}
\]

If \(\limsup t_j/D_j\le\tau<\pi\mu_0/a_0\), it follows that

\[
\liminf\bigl(D_j-D(n_j)\bigr)
\ge 2\mu_0-\frac{2a_0}{\pi}\tau>0.
\tag{4.2}
\]

Thus the chart need not cover every interior point, or even all but a sublinear
number: a small linear exception budget is permitted. A source with a larger
uncharted or interface population is not excluded by this argument.

For sources subject to fixed roundness and exception moduli tending to zero,
the theorem also implies existence of fixed positive tolerances for hull
perimeter-excess fraction and chart displacement, with strict gap at least
\(\delta_0/2\) once D is large. Otherwise diagonal choices with both
tolerances tending to zero would contradict (2.2). No numerical tolerance or
effective diameter threshold is claimed.

### 4.2 A necessary interior-outlier population for actual minimizers

The chart is allowed to cover any subset of the actual non-hull points. Put t_j
equal to the number it misses, with no size restriction. The argument leading
to (4.1) still applies. For global minimizers, translated-lattice averaging gives
\(n_j\ge\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)\). Consequently

\[
\boxed{\liminf_j\frac{t_j}{D_j}\ge
\frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}=0.3286099257\ldots.}
\tag{4.3}
\]

This holds for every allowed sequence of near-exact charts when the actual hull
perimeter excess is o(D). It concerns *interior* outliers, even though all hull
vertices were already allowed to be outliers. It does not infer quadratic
outlier population or a payment of these outliers into canonical detour currency.

### 4.3 Two physical grains separated by one straight interface

The mechanism extends to two genuinely different interior charts, provided the
actual source is partitioned by one straight cut. This is an additional proved
candidate, not an assumption that a canonical grain admits such a partition.

Retain the source hull and roundness hypotheses of §2. A line splits the hull
into two regions. Its normalized offset converges to a value strictly between
-1/2 and 1/2, so both limiting regions have positive area. Let L_j be its chord
length in K_j. Apart from t_j exceptions, the actual non-hull source points on
each side have an o(1)-error injective chart in a lattice of the same compact
family, independently on the two sides. Then

\[
\boxed{\liminf_j
\frac{\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)-n_j+L_j+t_j}{D_j}
\ge \frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}.}
\tag{4.4}
\]

If \(\limsup(L_j+t_j)/D_j\le\tau<\pi\mu_0/a_0\), there is a same-cardinality
strict competitor with

\[
\liminf(D_j-D(n_j))\ge2\mu_0-(2a_0/\pi)\tau>0.
\tag{4.5}
\]

The implication chain is

```text
actual two-chart partition + small physical cut/interface length
    -> inward forbidden-site payment minus the exact linear cut charge
    -> positive full-source population deficit
    -> strict same-cardinality triangular-lattice competitor
```

Proof. Fix the small parameters alpha and eta used in §3.8. Covered source
points away from bad sides lie in the erosion
\(Q=(K_j)_{-(a_0(1-\alpha)-\epsilon_j)}\). Their lattice shadows lie in the two
cut half-planes enlarged by epsilon. Denote the resulting two convex regions by
Q_+ and Q_-. Their overlap has area o(D), and
\(\operatorname{area}(Q_+)+\operatorname{area}(Q_-)=\operatorname{area}(Q)+o(D)\).
The cut chords of these regions have length \(L_j+o(D_j)\), since a bounded
erosion or o(1) line displacement changes a transverse macroscopic chord by o(D).

For either lattice, let H be the lattice-site hull in its cut region. Its
normalized limit is a circular segment, with exactly one straight boundary
segment, namely the limiting cut chord. The lattice boundary count satisfies

\[
B(H)\le L_j+o(D_j).
\tag{4.6}
\]

Here is the modification of §3.3 that proves it. First split primitive vectors
at a fixed length M. Long vectors contribute O(D/M). Among the finitely many
short directions, any side of H with linear length must converge to a straight
side of the limiting circular segment, so its direction must approach the
cut direction. Distinct bounded-length primitive directions have an angular
separation bounded below within the compact lattice family. Thus at most one
oriented short direction can converge to that cut side. Its physical length is
at most L_j+o(D); its lattice multiplicity is no larger, since the primitive
vector has length at least one. Every other short-direction side has length
o(D). Let D increase, then M increase. This proves (4.6). The H limit follows
by bounded covering radius and support approximation inside the positive-area
circular segment. More explicitly, that cut region contains a disk of radius
rho D for one rho>0. Move any boundary point a fraction O(1/D) toward this
disk's center. Convexity puts a disk of the lattice covering radius around the
moved point inside the region. A nearest lattice site then approximates the
original point within O(1), which proves the normalized H limit. Triangulation now gives a lattice count at most
\(\operatorname{area}(Q_\pm)/a_\pm+L_j/2+o(D_j)\).

Assign the forbidden patches of §3.8 to their cut side, skipping any patch
within bounded distance of the cut. The skipped sides number o(D): after
normalization they lie near one of the two points where the limiting cut
meets the circle, and the direction measure is uniform. Each cut side corresponds
to a fixed limiting angular interval. The joint equidistribution of §3.7 remains
valid restricted to that interval, by continuous inner/outer approximation of
its indicator; the two endpoint directions have zero angular measure. Thus on
side sigma, with q_sigma eligible hull sites,

\[
M_\sigma=q_\sigma\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)/a_\sigma+o(D_j),
\qquad q_++q_-=q_j+o(D_j).
\]

These are distinct excluded chart sites *inside* the corresponding counted
regions. Each region has quadratic area, so its area-minus-patch numerator
is positive. Replacing its covolume by a_0 is therefore valid. Summing the two
triangulation counts, adding the q hull vertices, all t exceptions, and the
bad-side o(D) population gives

\[
n_j\le \operatorname{area}(K_j)/a_0+\alpha P_j+L_j+t_j
       -q_j\operatorname{area}(F_\eta)/a_0+o(D_j).
\]

Taking limits in the same order as §3.8 proves (4.4); the independent disk
competitor proves (4.5). Both charts and all exceptions are actual source
populations, and no cross-chart compatibility of an independently rebuilt
source is assumed.

For an actual global minimizer in this two-chart class, (4.4) forces
\(\liminf(L_j+t_j)/D_j\ge\pi\mu_0/a_0\). A cut with longer interface is not
excluded by this estimate. In particular an interface of length comparable to
D can absorb the entire forbidden-strip gain. Dropping the interface term would
be wrong: a primitive lattice row on a flat boundary has linear boundary count.
For the triangular-lattice parallelogram with index rectangle 0<=i<=m,
0<=k<=s, the exact boundary lattice count is 2m+2s. The cut boundary cannot
be treated as a curved outer boundary with o(D) lattice sites.

### 4.4 The interface charge vanishes without lattice resonance

The cut charge in (4.4) can be made source-specific, rather than bounded only
by physical length. Pass to a subsequence on which the two lattice matrices and
the unit cut tangent converge. In limit lattice sigma, let ell_sigma be the
length of its primitive vector parallel to the limiting cut; set ell_sigma to
infinity if no nonzero lattice vector is parallel to that cut. Phases do not
enter this definition. Use the convention 1/infinity=0. Then

\[
\boxed{\liminf_j
\frac{\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)-n_j+t_j
 +(L_j/2)(1/\ell_++1/\ell_-)}{D_j}
\ge\frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}.}
\tag{4.7}
\]

In particular:

- If both limiting cut directions are nonresonant, the full gap 2mu_0 survives
  for every macroscopic cut length, when t=o(D).
- If one is nonresonant and the other has primitive length at least sqrt(3),
  the full-source gap is at least
  \(2\mu_0-1/(2\pi)>0.0220\), since \(L_j\le D_j\).
- If both primitive lengths are at least sqrt(13), a strict competitor exists
  for every cut length: \(1/\sqrt{13}<\pi\mu_0/a_0\).

Proof. In the proof of (4.6), keep the primitive vector's length instead of
replacing it by one. At any fixed cutoff M, a short direction can have a hull
side of linear length only if its limiting direction is exactly parallel to
the limiting cut. In a lattice there is at most one primitive direction with
that orientation. Its multiplicity is at most \(L_j/\ell_\sigma+o(D_j)\).
If the direction is nonresonant, all short-direction side lengths are o(D).
The long directions still contribute O(D/M), with a uniform constant. Taking
D to infinity and then M to infinity proves

\[
B(H_\sigma)\le L_j/\ell_\sigma+o(D_j).
\]

Use this bound in the triangulation count of §4.3. No change to the exclusion,
chart, or separation argument is necessary. This proves (4.7) and its strict
competitor conclusion whenever the retained weighted cut charge plus t is
less than \((\pi\mu_0/a_0)D\).

For actual global minimizers with two macroscopic chart domains and t=O(D),
each chart's covolume must tend to a_0, in fact with excess O(1/D). Indeed,
even the unsharpened chart counts and cut bounds give

\[
n_j\le q_j+\sum_\sigma\operatorname{area}(Q_\sigma)/a_{\sigma,j}
       +O(D_j)+t_j.
\]

Each Q_sigma has area bounded below by a fixed positive multiple of D^2;
its total area is at most pi D^2/4+o(D), with its actual erosion retained.
Combining with \(n_j\ge\pi D_j^2/(4a_0)\) bounds
\(\sum\operatorname{area}(Q_\sigma)(1/a_0-1/a_{\sigma,j})\) by O(D).
Both nonnegative covolume excesses are therefore O(1/D). Since lambda>=1
and sin(theta)>=a_0, each limiting chart is a unit triangular lattice, with
angle pi/3 or 2pi/3 and inflation one. This conclusion does not assert a
triangular contact in the actual o(1)-displaced source.

Thus the primitive lengths in surviving limiting interfaces have the
triangular-lattice form sqrt(m^2+n^2+mn), with gcd(m,n)=1, or the equivalent
minus-sign form. Generic cut directions already close. A long interface can
survive this estimate only through sufficiently short rational lattice rows
or a sufficiently large explicit exception population. This is a real
restriction on a physical two-chart source, not a statement about an abstract
phase crossing graph.

### 4.5 An exact remaining seam inequality

The coarse length L and its refined primitive-row charge are upper bounds.
The next obstruction can be expressed as an exact discrete population ledger.
For each cut region Q_sigma, let S_sigma be all sites of its chart in that
region, let M_sigma count the disjoint forbidden patch sites of §3.7, and let
N_sigma count actual covered source points, using their injective shadows.
Define

\[
E_\sigma=|S_\sigma|-\operatorname{area}(Q_\sigma)/a_\sigma,
\qquad
J=\sum_\sigma(|S_\sigma|-M_\sigma-N_\sigma)\ge0.
\]

Here the fixed positive patch trimming and vanishing chart margins are retained
until D tends to infinity; uncovered bad-side points are included in the o(D)
error. The exact identity is
\(\sum N_\sigma=\sum\operatorname{area}(Q_\sigma)/a_\sigma
 +E_++E_--M_+-M_--J\).
Repeating the positive-numerator comparison gives the sufficient closure

\[
\limsup\frac{E_++E_--J+t}{D}<\frac{\pi\mu_0}{a_0}.
\tag{4.8}
\]

A stronger, especially clean seam theorem would be
\(E_++E_--J\le o(D)\) for the actual two-chart source under its cross-interface
unit-separation constraints. Section 4.6 proves it for exactly parallel
periods in {1,sqrt(3),sqrt(7)}, both equal and mixed. It is not proved for every resonant interface. It cannot be replaced by J>=L: for two
identical charts, the artificial cut charge may already cancel in E_++E_-
without any additional missing site. Such a replacement would demand holes
that a single feasible lattice does not have. The new open proposition must
charge the actual count deviation E, rather than the worst-case cut length.

### 4.6 Exact parallel rows of equal period 1 or sqrt(3) close

There is a provable seam cancellation in two important resonant cases. Retain
the two-chart physical source setup. Suppose both charts have a primitive
vector **exactly parallel to the actual cut**, their covolumes tend to a_0,
and these two primitive lengths tend to the same
\(\ell\in\{1,\sqrt3\}\). This assumption is stronger than limit parallelism;
no uncontrolled rate of cut-angle convergence is used. Then

\[
\boxed{E_++E_--J\le o(D).}
\tag{4.9}
\]

Consequently the full strict competitor gap 2mu_0 survives for any macroscopic
cut length when t=o(D); the linear exception version survives as in (4.2).
For hypothetical global minimizers the required covolume limits are automatic
by §4.4, but exact cut parallelism is still an additional hypothesis.

Proof. Write the actual primitive lengths as ell_sigma and their normal row
pitches as \(h_\sigma=a_\sigma/\ell_\sigma\). Thus
\(h_\sigma\to h=a_0/\ell\). Let d_sigma in [0,h_sigma) be the distance
from the enlarged cut half-plane boundary to its nearest lattice row inside
that region. Moving that boundary inward by d_sigma does not remove a lattice
site: there are no lattice rows in the open gap. The new clipped convex region
has area smaller by \(d_\sigma L_j+o(D_j)\). Its cut lattice boundary count is
at most \(L_j/\ell_\sigma+o(D_j)\), by §4.4. Triangulation therefore gives

\[
E_\sigma\le\frac{L_j}{a_\sigma}(h_\sigma/2-d_\sigma)+o(D_j).
\tag{4.10}
\]

All row distances are bounded. The actual separation g_j of the two outermost
rows, measured normal to the original cut, is
\(d_++d_--2\epsilon_j\). It is at least -2epsilon and has bounded size.
Equation (4.10) consequently yields

\[
E_++E_-\le(L_j/a_0)(h-g_j)+o(D_j).
\tag{4.11}
\]

Pass to any subsequence on which g_j has a limit g>=0. If g>=h, (4.11) is
already at most o(D), and J>=0 proves (4.9). If g<h, retain a fixed positive
margin below h. Match points on the two outermost ideal chart rows by nearest
tangential coordinate. The row periods both tend to ell. Removing duplicate
nearest matches costs \(O(L_j|\ell_+-\ell_-|+1)=o(D_j)\) sites. Endpoint
truncation also costs o(D), since both row intersections have length L_j+o(D).
Thus there are \(L_j/\ell-o(D_j)\) disjoint matched pairs, with tangential
separation at most ell/2+o(1). Row sites in the forbidden outer-hull patches
number o(D), since the macroscopic cut meets the circle transversely at only
two points; discard them as well. All remaining missing row sites contribute
to J rather than to the already paid M_sigma.

For ell=1 or sqrt(3), precisely,

\[
h^2+(\ell/2)^2=a_0^2/\ell^2+\ell^2/4=1.
\]

Since g<h by a fixed margin, both source points of a matched pair cannot
exist: their shadow separation is less than one, and the vanishing chart errors
preserve the strict inequality. Each pair therefore removes at least one ideal
site. The injective charts and disjoint matching imply
\(J\ge L_j/\ell-o(D_j)\). Combining with (4.11) gives

\[
E_++E_--J
\le (L_j/a_0)(h-g)-L_j/\ell+o(D_j)
= -g L_j/a_0+o(D_j)\le o(D_j).
\]

This proves (4.9) on every subsequence, including the limiting case g=h handled
by the first alternative. The extra holes are forced by actual cross-interface
source separation, not assumed from independently assembled half-lattices.
The independent disk construction then verifies the strict competitor.

The matching rate assertion can also be seen directly: two increasing
arithmetic progressions of periods tending to ell have counts differing by
o(D) on a length-O(D) interval. Nearest-index rounding is monotone. Its repeats
are bounded by the period-ratio deficit times the interval length plus endpoint
errors. Deleting those repeats leaves the claimed injective nearest matching.

The remaining seam cases involve higher primitive periods or limit-parallel
cuts without exact parallelism. The mixed cases and sqrt(7) extension are
proved below. The full matching
argument above does not cover those hypotheses. A supplementary full-row
mirror pilot tested primitive norms 1, sqrt(3), sqrt(7), sqrt(13), sqrt(19).
It found the expected limiting gaps h in the first two cases and larger gaps
in the others; those floating-point values do not prove the remaining cases
or an arbitrary-hole charge.

#### Mixed periods 1 and sqrt(3) also close

Assume both primitive rows are exactly parallel to the actual cut, with limiting
periods 1 and sqrt(3), respectively. The limiting pitches are a_0 and 1/2.
The same first-row clipping argument gives

\[
E_++E_-\le L\left(\frac{1+1/\sqrt3}{2}-\frac g{a_0}\right)+o(D).
\tag{4.12}
\]

Let \(\bar h=(a_0+1/2)/2\). If g>=bar_h, this is nonpositive. If g<bar_h,
there are two matching alternatives that supply the missing-site payment:

- For 0<=g<=1/4, use the first **two** ideal rows of the sqrt(3)-period chart.
  In the limiting unit triangular lattice their tangential phases differ by
  sqrt(3)/2, and their normal heights differ by 1/2. For example, take primitive
  direction (1,1) and companion (0,1); their tangential projection is sqrt(3)/2
  and normal projection 1/2. Symmetries give the same statement for every
  sqrt(3) primitive direction. The two-row union therefore has alternating
  tangential gaps tending to sqrt(3)/2=a_0<1. Every unit-row site can be matched
  injectively to its nearest tangential site of this union, up to o(D) endpoint
  errors: the nearest-site cells have width tending to a_0, less than the
  unit-row period. The tangent separation is at most sqrt(3)/4+o(1), and the
  normal separation at most g+1/2+o(1)<=3/4+o(1). Thus the squared distance is
  at most 3/16+9/16+o(1)=3/4+o(1)<1. Each pair forces a missing site.
  Consequently J>=L-o(D), while the coefficient in (4.12) is at most
  (1+1/sqrt(3))/2<1. This pays the entire possible positive count deviation.
- For 1/4<g<bar_h, match just the first sqrt(3)-period row to nearest sites of
  the unit-period row. That matching is injective because the former period
  is larger, and supplies L/sqrt(3)-o(D) pairs. The tangent separation is at
  most 1/2+o(1), and g<bar_h<a_0; hence each pair still has distance less
  than one by a fixed limiting margin. At g=1/4 the coefficient in (4.12)
  is exactly 1/2, since 1/(2sqrt(3))=1/(4a_0); it decreases thereafter.
  Since 1/sqrt(3)>1/2, the missing-site payment again exceeds that deviation.

The row-phase and period assertions remain valid for the vanishing chart
perturbations because the limiting chart is triangular. As in the equal-period
proof, remove o(D) sites at endpoints or the already-paid outer patches; every
retained deleted site is charged to J. These alternatives prove
E_++E_--J<=o(D), including the threshold cases by subsequences. Thus both mixed
short-period interfaces close as well. A single outer-row matching would have
failed at very small g: its L/sqrt(3) payment is below the possible coefficient
(1+1/sqrt(3))/2. The two-row union supplies the missing global charge.

#### The sqrt(7) primitive period: equal and both mixed cases close

The short-period seam theorem extends to every pair of periods in
\(\{1,\sqrt3,\sqrt7\}\), under the same exact-parallel and triangular-limit
hypotheses. The following proof treats the additional cases.

Put \(b=\sqrt7\), \(h=a_0/b\), and \(d=5/(2b)\). In a unit triangular
lattice, a primitive direction of length b is a symmetry of index (2,1).
With companion (1,1), its projected row phase is \(9/(2b)\), and its normal
pitch is h. Thus the two first rows have alternating tangent gaps
\(d=5/(2b)\) and \(b-d=9/(2b)\). Rotations and reflections either preserve
or interchange these gaps. Completeness is elementary: norm
\(m^2+n^2+mn=7\) implies \(m^2+n^2\le14\), so |m|,|n|<=3; the twelve
solutions are precisely the hexagonal rotation/reflection orbit of (2,1).
The exact script enumerates this finite set. All phases below mean phases
modulo their primitive period, and vanishing chart errors are treated as in
§4.6.

**Periods b and b.** Their possible positive count deviation is at most
\((L/a_0)(h-g)+o(D)\). If g>=h there is nothing to pay. Otherwise retain a
fixed limiting margin g<h. For each site of the first chart's outermost row,
let tau in [-b/2,b/2] be its nearest phase difference from the other chart's
outermost row. If |tau|<=d, pair those first-row sites. The squared distance
bound is strictly below
\(d^2+h^2=25/28+3/28=1\).
If |tau|>d, use the other chart's next row. Its relative phase is shifted by
+d or -d. For every such tau the new closest tangent difference is at most
2/b: opposite signs give at most b/2-d=1/b; equal signs wrap modulo b and
give at most 2/b. The squared distance is strictly below
\((2/b)^2+(2h)^2=4/7+3/7=1\).
Nearest-index maps to each target row have only o(D) repeats because their
period ratio tends to one. Discard these repeats and endpoints. This provides
L/b-o(D) disjoint pairs, even when the row choice varies with tau.
Therefore J>=L/b-o(D), paying the maximum deviation L/b. This closes b,b.

**Periods sqrt(3) and b.** Their possible deviation is at most
\(L[(1/\sqrt3+1/b)/2-g/a_0]+o(D)\). Let
\(\bar h=(1/2+h)/2<1/2\). If g>=bar_h it is nonpositive. For g<=1/6,
match the sqrt(3)-period outer row to nearest tangent sites of the other
chart's first two rows. The union's nearest-site cells have width tending
to b/2<sqrt(3), hence the matching is injective. Tangent differences are at
most 9/(4b), and normal differences at most g+h. The worst squared bound is

\[
(9/(4b))^2+(1/6+h)^2<1.
\]

This supplies J>=L/sqrt(3)-o(D), exceeding the maximum possible deviation.
For 1/6<g<bar_h, match just the b-period first row to the other first row.
The tangent bound is sqrt(3)/2, and g<bar_h<1/2, so pairs have distance <1.
The matching is injective and supplies L/b-o(D). At g=1/6 the deviation
coefficient is already less than 1/b, and decreases with g. This closes
sqrt(3),b in either orientation.

**Periods 1 and b.** For g>=\(\bar h=(a_0+h)/2<a_0\), the deviation is
nonpositive. For g<=1/3, match *both* b-period rows to nearest sites of the
unit-period first row. Every prospective pair has distance <1, since its
tangent difference is at most 1/2 and its normal difference at most
1/3+h<a_0. Some target repeats must be removed, and they must be counted
rather than assumed negligible.

A repeat occurs only at the short gap d<1 between two consecutive sites
of the b-period two-row union. The other gap exceeds one. As the periods
advance, the phase of that pair modulo the unit period is uniformly
distributed, even for adversarial shifts. Here is the required elementary
uniformity: for every fixed nonzero integer k, the normalized exponential
sum of an arithmetic progression with step rho_j tending to the irrational
number b is at most
\(2/(N|1-e^{2\pi i k\rho_j}|)\to0\). Indicator approximation applies to the
nearest-index repeat interval, of limiting length 1-d. Thus repeats number
\((1-d)L/b+o(D)\). Keeping one pair per target yields

\[
J\ge\frac{1+d}{b}L-o(D)
=\left(\frac1b+\frac5{14}\right)L-o(D).
\]

This exceeds the maximum deviation coefficient (1+1/b)/2, because their
difference is 1/(2b)-1/7>0. For 1/3<g<bar_h, match only the b-period first
row to nearest unit-row sites. That matching is injective and has distance
<1 since g<bar_h<a_0. It supplies L/b-o(D) holes, while the deviation
coefficient at g=1/3 is already less than 1/b. This closes 1,b in either
orientation.

All required scalar margins and squared-distance identities are checked
outwardly with Fraction bounds in the exact script. The phase progression
argument counts the repeats that a naive nearest matching would have missed;
it does not rely on a random row translation. Near outer endpoints discard
o(D) sites, as before, so these additional missing sites are outside the
already-paid forbidden patches. The row strips have bounded physical depth,
allowing their charges to be summed in the finite-atlas proof below.

### 4.7 A fixed finite physical atlas: the seam charges really add

The two-grain payment is compatible across a fixed finite partition. This is
an additional source theorem, not an identification with the canonical
contact/phase grain decomposition.

Assume the source hull, roundness, and perimeter-excess hypotheses of §2.
Partition the actual hull into a fixed finite number of convex cells using
straight segments. The normalized partition converges to a partition of the
disk into positive-area convex cells with finitely many interfaces. Assume
its macroscopic interface/vertex pattern is stable, with nonzero incident
angles, and that interface endpoints on the outer circle are transverse.
Each actual non-hull source point, except t_j=o(D_j) points, has a vanishing-error
injective chart belonging to its physical cell. All charts belong to the compact
family of §2 and their covolumes tend to a_0. For every internal interface,
assume one of:

- its limiting tangent is nonresonant in both adjacent limiting lattices; or
- both adjacent lattices have primitive rows exactly parallel to the actual
  interface, with each limiting period in {1,sqrt(3),sqrt(7)}, either equal or mixed.

Then the source has a same-cardinality strict competitor with
\(\liminf(D_j-D(n_j))\ge2\mu_0\).
For an actual global-minimizer sequence, each positive-area chart's covolume
limit is automatically a_0 by the leading-area comparison of §4.4. Thus this
atlas class is excluded from global minimizers. No source chart is inferred
from an abstract harmonic grain.

Proof. Erode the hull and enlarge each cell half-plane by the chart error,
as in §4.3. The sum of cell-region areas equals the eroded hull area plus
o(D), since finitely many overlaps have vanishing width and O(D) total length.
Assign each forbidden outer patch to its physical cell. Only o(D) patches
meet cell interfaces: after normalization their locations approach finitely
many interface endpoints on the circle. Joint equidistribution on each of
the finitely many outer angular intervals gives the same total forbidden
strip payment as §3.7, with each chart's own covolume.

For each cell define S, M, N, E and its additional missing-site count as in
§4.5. The normalized lattice-site hull is that cell's limiting convex region.
The short-primitive-vector argument of §4.4 bounds its boundary-site count
by the sum of L/ell over its resonant straight faces, plus o(D); its curved
outer faces and nonresonant straight faces contribute o(D). At exactly parallel
resonant faces, clip each supporting line to the first ideal lattice row.
Every clip preserves all lattice sites. The area loss is the sum of d L over
those faces plus O(1), because the finite vertex angles stay nonzero. Thus

\[
E_r\le\sum_{e\text{ resonant at }r}
\frac{L_e}{a_r}(h_{r,e}/2-d_{r,e})+o(D).
\]

All cell covolumes tend to a_0. Group the two contributions from each interface
and apply the alternative in §4.6. If the opposing rows are too close, the
one-row or two-row matching of §4.6 forces enough unused ideal sites to pay
that interface's count deviation. If they are not too close, its count deviation
is already nonpositive to o(D).
Nonresonant interfaces have no linear count deviation.

These missing-site charges can be summed. Away from bounded neighborhoods of
the finitely many interface vertices, the bounded-depth row strips of
different interfaces are disjoint: separate macroscopic edges have positive
scaled distance, and edges meeting at a vertex have a fixed nonzero angle.
Ideal lattice sites in the excluded vertex neighborhoods number O(1), by the
uniform minimum separation. Outer-patch intersections at the finitely many
outer endpoints cost o(D). After removing these sites, a matched chart site
belongs to only one interface. Consequently the global additional missing-site
count J is at least the sum of all retained row-deletion charges, with error
o(D). This is a simultaneous count on the same actual source; no one-at-a-time
exchange or independent grain reconstruction is being combined.

It follows that \(\sum_r E_r-J\le o(D)\). The exact global count identity is
\(\sum_r N_r=\sum_r\operatorname{area}(Q_r)/a_r+\sum_r E_r-\sum_r M_r-J\).
Subtracting M_r before replacing each covolume by a_0 is justified by each
cell's positive quadratic area. The erosion cancels the hull-site count, and
we obtain

\[
n_j\le\operatorname{area}(K_j)/a_0-(\pi\mu_0/a_0)D_j+o(D_j).
\]

The independent translated triangular-lattice disk proves the stated strict
diameter gap. The same argument retains a linear t budget with the margin
(4.2). Fixed finiteness, nondegenerate cell geometry, actual charts, and the
specified interface hypotheses are necessary scope restrictions here.

## 5. Scope, regressions, and failed attacks

| Claim or attack | Classification | Exact disposition |
| --- | --- | --- |
| (2.1) and (2.2) | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | Complete proof above; independent audit still needed |
| Fixed finite physical atlas with the specified seams (4.7) | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | Missing-site charges add on disjoint interface strips; full-source strict competitor |
| Exact parallel periods in {1,sqrt(3),sqrt(7)}, equal or mixed (§4.6) | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | Actual separation forces the seam payment; full strict competitor gap |
| Nonresonant or sufficiently long primitive cut rows (4.7) | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | Exact source competitor; no physical short-cut hypothesis needed |
| Two actual chart grains with short cut (4.4)–(4.5) | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | Full source competitor when cut plus exceptions is below 0.3286099 D |
| Necessary interior outliers (4.3) | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | At least 0.3286099 D misses from any one chart, under the perimeter-excess hypothesis |
| Global corollary with almost unit hull and one near-exact interior chart | Proved candidate awaiting promotion | Uses only the canonical full-source roundness/Oler inputs listed in §4 |
| Unit-shell conclusion with arbitrary uncharted interior | Unproved | The o(D) lattice count and its missing-site trace have no established replacement |
| One triangular-lattice competitor per cardinality proves Level I | Rejected inference | Our conclusion is only exclusion of the class in §2 |
| Generic translation of the old source | Rejected tactic | The old chart may have adversarial phase; §3.4 treats every translation |
| Leading area comparison for a unit ring | Insufficient | The shell population exactly cancels the a_0 erosion term at a=a_0; the inward-trace missing sites supply the strict loss |
| Refilling old vacant sites | Insufficient | Dense sources can have no unit-clear insertion site; the new source is independently constructed |
| Straightening the same lattice indices | Not used | PR #582 shows this can increase diameter; the proof changes the index set |

Specific regression checks:

- **Regular prime shell local minima:** preserved. The theorem compares a distant
  configuration and does not assert an infinitesimal or finite local release.
- **Dense regular-shell filling:** now excluded from global optimality when its
  actual interior has one exact or o(1)-error lattice chart. Leading density and
  exact KKT stress alone remain insufficient.
- **Contact-isolated source points:** all are included in n, and have shadows or
  are charged to the explicit exception budget.
- **Actual source versus carrier:** no carrier is used in the proof.
- **Simultaneous exchanges:** none are used; the competitor is verified as a whole.
- **Boundary-order losses:** every loss from chart error, offset, lattice hull
  boundary, and equidistribution is o(D); the retained missing-site gain is cD.
- **Star/non-Eulerian diameter graphs:** no diameter-graph topology is assumed.
- **Repeated outer-walk vertices or bridges:** no contact outer walk is used.
- **RSR equality uniqueness and inward-pocket containment:** neither is imported.

## 6. Exact remaining lemma and frontier effect

No canonical state file is changed. The report excludes a restricted source
class, including the prominent regular-shell/lattice-interior model. It does not
eliminate the macroscopic-container branch, the high-surplus full-core branch,
or an entire canonical Target A/B/C endpoint.

A precise next obligation for this lane is the following unit-hull capacity
statement, requiring genuinely new source information:

> There is c>0 such that every sequence of triangle-free global minimizers with
> D_j tending to infinity and (P_j-q_j)/D_j tending to zero satisfies
> liminf (area(conv A_j)/a_0-n_j)/D_j >= c.

Together with the independent translated-lattice competitor this is an exact
contradiction. This report proves it under the one-chart interior hypothesis.
Removing that hypothesis requires a payment for actual multigrain/interface
population; it cannot follow from generic local jamming or from regular-shell
stress. Sources with perimeter excess \(P-q=\Theta(D)\) still need a separate argument.

Sections 4.3–4.4 supply the intermediate inequality for two actual convex
grains separated by a straight cut, and eliminate the nonresonant interface
class. The sharper remaining obligation is the exact seam ledger (4.8),
particularly E_++E_--J<=o(D) for surviving higher-period or limit-parallel resonant rows. Exactly parallel
cut periods in {1,sqrt(3),sqrt(7)}, including mixed periods, are closed by §4.6, and §4.7 sums those payments across a fixed finite
physical atlas with stable geometry. One cannot replace
actual count deviation E by a presumed missing-site charge J>=L. No universal
resonant seam cancellation is asserted here.

## 7. Computation and reproducibility

`research/check_unit_hull_lattice_boundary_loss.py` performs two supplementary
checks. Its exact `Fraction` mode verifies rational lower bounds on r_0, uses a
Machin/alternating-series pi enclosure, and checks both positive competitor
margins and the full-strip exception threshold. Its numerical mode enumerates regular-shell lattice
fillings and adversarial translations/rotations, compares their cardinalities
with the disk translation-average competitor, and measures inward-trace
characters. Floating-point output is discovery/regression evidence only; it
does not certify global optimality or prove the asymptotic theorem.

The numerical pilot covered q=101, 401, 1001, and 5001 at three rotations and
translations, with chart angle excess q^-2 and inflation 1+q^-3. At q=5001
it counted 2,297,515–2,297,617 source points, 531–567 omitted sites inside the
eroded disk, and average-disk competitor gaps 0.17518–0.21052. One finite
gap is below the limiting guarantee; the theorem supplies no finite threshold.
The exact constant check passed with diameter margin >0.18117214 and interior
exception threshold >0.3286099. See the compact JSON summary under research/.

